A||||13||TFsKMA||i need help||||1||
Z||000000||TFsKMA||01-25-2001||05:34 PM||||I am supposed to be getting an '87 Fiero GT in April or May from Ed in Alabama with a 3.4 and a 4.10 tranny.  I might not be able to save up enough money and my dad may co-sign for a loan if he knows some info.  He wants to know how reliable a Fiero is, and how easy it is to get the parts if I needed them.  Please post any info on the Fiero safety and reliability.  I want this car and I'm afriad my dad won't co-sign for the loan without any information.<BR>thanks a bunch<BR>Andy<P>------------------<BR>Too Fast so Kiss My A$$||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||01-25-2001||07:04 PM||||The reliability of a Fiero is directly proportional to your driving and maintainance habits. If you get a Fiero from Ed Parks it most likely will be in very good shape, BUT and I may be wrong, you sound like a fairly new driver and you are contemplating a performance enhanced car. If you drive it hard it will break. Parts are plentiful and not overly expensive, finding someone willing to work on it will the hard part. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||01-25-2001||07:37 PM||||I'll have to agree with Phil.  Ed should make sure the car is ready to roll.  But with any older car there may be some things that may get overlooked.  Working today, but breaking tomorrow.  Unfortunate, but not unavoidable.   Have a good local mechanic around will be helpful.  The 3.4 swap will be  much more powerful than a stock 2.8 and if you are an inexperienced driver, you can easily over drive your car.  The Fieros do hold the road real well, but can break loose in some driving conditions.  Only advise, you will have a fun car to drive.  Don't think you are Mario Andretti with it on the road.  Take your time to get used to how the car handles.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Raydar||01-26-2001||12:11 AM||||What they said...<BR>With Ed, it's pretty much "what you see is what you get". If there is anything that needs attention, he will likely fix it, or at least let you know before you leave with it. (Some people prefer to do their own minor repairs.) <BR>If I were going to buy a Fiero from someone other than a private individual, I wouldn't hesitate to go to Ed. If I ever do a 3.4 swap, he's going to be my first choice.<BR>A Fiero will be as reliable as any 13-17 year old car.<BR>If you beat on it, it'll probably let you down. If you maintain it, it will be reliable. <BR>Most parts are still available. If nowhere else, look in the Mall on this Forum. There's always somebody parting out a car. Most of the drivetrain parts are just run of the mill GM.<BR>It's true that many mechanics don't like to work on Fieros, but with a little ingenuity (and a little help from the Forum community), you can fix most anything that goes wrong.<BR>Just my $.02.<P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<P><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||MooCow||01-26-2001||12:22 AM||||Look at my car that I just got back from Ed.  I have the same set up as you will be getting.  Only thing wrong with my car is the paint and it being dirty from sitting forever.  Other than that Ed did everything I wanted him to do.  I still needed some things wrong with the car or what would I work on.  The Fiero to me is a hobby and slowly starting to become more in my life.  As soon as I move out I am going to start collecting them and fixing them and show them off.  Sorry for the long ramble and sorta getting off topic.  Trust me Ed does very nice work and won't do you wrong.<BR>If you ever want another test drive just let me know.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>                           Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000005||tgowens||01-26-2001||03:03 AM||||I agree with what Old Lar and Phil said as well as the others. I know Ed and will certainly tell you that he will set the car up right, He is also invaluable when it comes to advice on what to fix should anything go wrong. Here at the Fiero Shop we work with Ed on a number of projects and I know him well enough to know that that car will be running right when it leaves his shop.<P>Now, no one has mentioned anything about safety.......the Fiero is built around a "space frame" -- here is a shot........<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-002f_7.jpg">  <BR>This means that you are virtually surrounded by a steel "roll cage".........the body panels are enduroflex.........The body was designed to "accordian" in an accident....the front and rear bumpers have a shock absorber attached to them (under the front and rear fascias (bumper covers) to absorb shock in an impact. and........during the five years the Fiero was produced, it ranked second to the Volvo in safety tests, outranking the BMW and Mercedes..........one thing you should know though, the insurance companies look at these cars as being two seat sports cars (which they are) making them somewhat higher in insurance rates than the safety tests should make them..........<P>As for parts availability - there are many throughout this country (Ed Parks, us, Kick Hill, FieroDave, etc.........) who can provide you with parts for the parts no longer manufactured by GM. <P>Now, I am a 53 year old man who is rebuilding an '87GT for my youngest daughter as well as an '87 GT for my oldest daughter.........besides that, my own personal car is an '87GT !!<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||RaeQwan||01-26-2001||03:08 AM||||I love fieros.. just figured I'd put that in.. its not just a car.. Its a Fiero..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Fun <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Ray - AIM: WookieeNC<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Fiero Coupe</A> - <A HREF="http://www.CarolinaFieros.com" TARGET=_blank>Carolina Fieros</A> - <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/dearabu/" TARGET=_blank>Dear Abu</A> - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.net" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Network</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||TFsKMA||01-26-2001||09:28 AM||||Hey thanks a bunch i think it may have helped.  But after reading the first 2 or 3 he said "see they said right there that they will break, so what do you want one for"  But I think I may have got the point across to him.  Umm Ken when I come over today to help i want a ride :-D  (My dad is just confusing and confused.)(he sees what he wants to see)  heheh<BR>Any more info would be great<BR>Andy<P>------------------<BR>Too Fast so Kiss My A$$||||reg||1
Z||000008||Got2manyfieros||01-26-2001||03:55 PM||||So I have this '84 se, and I have a nasty habit of driving it REALLY abusively, stopping abruptly, cornering hard, etc...  It's 17yrs old now, and I haven't had any major driveline failures (yet), I would say that my biggest annoyance thusfar is the headlight motors.  The 3.4 is a far better engine than the 2.5, in that aspect, you are better off.  <P>So I wouldn't worry about breaking the car, just don't beat on it all the time.  As long as you're good to it with the fluid changes (don't forget the tranny fluid) and keep good tires under it, you shouldn't have too much trouble.<P>good luck<BR>-Todd||||reg||1
Z||000009||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-26-2001||08:26 PM||||I am working to undo the damage of a first driver neglecting and abusing a Fiero.  <BR>The chassis was not lubed regularly, so a lot of the end links and ball joints are borderline.  The body is adorned with scratches and cracked panels.  This includes a driver side mirror that is bent forward and inward from some minor collision.  The head gasket (2.5L 4 cylinder) was blown because the thermostat was stuck and ignored.  The front wheel bearings were both very loose, and the passenger side was shot.  <P>I could go on, but I think you can see my point.  A 14 to 17 year old car cannot cope with neglect.  The car I have became unstable and difficult to drive as a result of neglect on chassis lubes, stuts/shocks/ and repacking the wheel bearings.  The engine suffered from neglect also as many of the sensors and gasket were past their usefull life or improperly installed.<P>I got a great deal on the car and I was looking for a hobby.  This is my third vehicle.  So, I can cope with breakdowns and extended periods of repair work without consequence.  <P>If you buy a Fiero, then do it because you like to work on cars and you are willing to do the job right.  If you want to impress your friends, this may not be the best choice as Fieros are an acquired taste and not generally accepted as desireable cars.  If they were, we would all be paying a lot more for them.  Lastly, if you want to "rat race" around town, you need a four door like a delta 88, caprice, or crown victoria.  Cops and taxi drivers know what kind of car likes to be driven hard and totally neglected.  <P>The best first car is one that gets you around and has room for all your friends to come along. Later, when you are a crusty old guy, you will be ready to savor a two-seater sports car.  Till then, drive something that can get you and your friends to the parties.<P>Oh yes.  Please let your dad know that I am not suggesting you go to parties or cruise around.  The advice contained in the response regarding having a lot of fun is to be taken at your own risk.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||01-27-2001||12:59 PM||||make sure you point out to him theres no room inside for open containers too !!||||reg||1
Z||000011||TFsKMA||01-27-2001||05:56 PM||||I want a 2 seater so I don't have to take all my friends all over.  Unless they pay some gas money. hehe.  <P>------------------<BR>Too Fast so Kiss My A$$||||reg||1
Z||000012||Motorhead||01-27-2001||09:07 PM||||I haven't owned anything with over 2 seats as a daily driver in over 20 years.  But that is strictly because of my preference for real sports cars.<BR>Any car which is over 5 years old begins a decline in performance simply because of its age.  Normal maintenance is to be expected.  If you're looking strictly for reliability, go out and buy a used late model "oriental" brand, Honda and Toyota jump to mind.  Or even a Ford or Chevy!<BR>  If you LOVE CARS,  pick a different criteria!  People who can't work on their own car or aren't rich don't drive Triumphs or Fiats or any "unusual" car!<BR>In other words, there's transportation!  Then there's "TRANSPORTATION"!<BR>My Fieros' fall into the latter catagory.  But then I do my own maintenance.<BR>Only you can judge.<BR>My 2 cents!<BR>Mike<BR>   <P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10234998&p=38686771&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||G-Nasty||01-28-2001||11:48 PM||||Fieros are problematic cars. Something will always happen or need attention. I've had 6 of the plastic pontiac cars and because of that unfortunate fact I have become an extremely well rounded mechanic.They are pretty safe- however the lack of airbags will leave you with sore ribs...believe me. In the rain, these cars suck. Unless you have ZR rated tires, I believe these cars were discontinued for thier high mortality rates and poor wet weather drivability.  ||||reg||1
